.-
help for ^latable^
.-

LaTable: Simplifies creation of LaTeX tables for data factsheets 
----------------------------------------

^latable^ ^using^ ^,^ ^table^(string) ^n^(numlist integer) [rowcolors(string)] 
[year(numlist integer)] [year1(numlist integer)] [year2(numlist integer)] 
[release(numlist integer)] [printdate(string)] [geounit(string)] [geo1(string)] [geo2(string)]

Description
-----------

^latable^ simplifies the creation of data factsheets in LaTeX by ensuring that data elements are 
formatted as desired (e.g., numbers taken to the correct number of decimal places) and wrapped with 
code to create tables with the necessary formatting (e.g., indentation to show heirarchy of categories).

latable requires Stata 16 or higher, as it requires two dataframes in memory at once: one with the 
table specs, the other with the data themselves.

Options
--------

Required options (an oxymoron, I know)

	^using^ provides the path to the file containing the table specs. The table spec file 
	(see sample) provides information such as what data element occupies each cell and how
	that data element should be formatted.
	
	^n^ identifies the observation for which the data are to be reported.

Optional options:

	^rowcolors^ defines color(s) to be used for table rows, which allows for interleaving of 
	row colors. The row background will be white if this option is not specified.
	
	^year^ is required if the table specs are year-agnostic but the data fields have the year 
	appended to their names. 
	
	^year1^ and ^year2^ support cases where the same field exists for multiple time points, 
	in wide format e.g., when creating a table that compares two time points
	
	^release^ can be used to differentiate 1-year from 5-year ACS data
	
	^printdate^ defines how date information should appear in the table header row (if desired).
	For example, "2025-26 School Year" 
	
	^geounit^ should be used in conjunction with `geounit' in the table specifications. For 
	example, a job flows table might want to say "County residents employed within the County"
	
	^geo1^ and ^geo2^ are useful when creating a table comparing two geographies. For example, 
	if comparing a neighborhood to the city as a whole, geo1 would take the neighborhood name
	and geo2 would take a value like "Gotham Citywide"
	
	



